{
  "title": "Bidirectional link (circulators)",
  "group": "Links & receivers",
  "description": "Full-duplex link where a 3-port optical circulator at each end (models/optical_field/circulator.va, routes 1->2->3->1) separates the two counter-propagating beams that share the link: node A transmits into its port 1, the line hangs off port 2, and the wave returning from node B drops out of port 3 into A's receiver — and vice-versa at B. Each propagation direction is a directed waveguide (WGF forward A->B, WGB backward B->A) — the simulator's honest split of the forward and backward fields on one bidirectional span. Because the circulators are non-reciprocal, each receiver recovers only the FAR transmitter's data (VPA ~500 Mb/s, VPB ~333 Mb/s) at ~40 dB of directional isolation. Drop each circulator's iso_db to watch the near-end transmitter bleed into its own receiver.",
  "analysis": {
    "mode": "transient",
    "t_stop": 8e-09,
    "points": 800,
    "solver": "bdf2"
  },
  "schematic": {
    "notes": [
      {
        "x": 40,
        "y": -150,
        "title": "Bidirectional link with circulators — what this testbench does",
        "lines": [
          "One shared fibre span carries BOTH directions at once. Circulator",
          "CA (node A) and CB (node B) each route light 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 1:",
          "  - transmit: MZM light enters port 1, leaves port 2 onto the line",
          "  - receive:  the wave arriving on port 2 leaves port 3 -> photodiode",
          "MZMA (driven by VPA, 500 Mb/s) sends A->B on the forward fibre WGF;",
          "MZMB (VPB, 333 Mb/s) sends B->A on the backward fibre WGB. Because the",
          "circulators are non-reciprocal, voutB recovers VPA and voutA recovers",
          "VPB — each end hears only the other end, off one fibre pair.",
          "",
          "Drop CA.iso_db / CB.iso_db from 40 to ~10 dB and re-run: the near-end",
          "transmitter now leaks into its own receiver (self-interference)."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "instances": {
